{
  "image_name": "4c5af5d7173f37ef43e15c8f33132d5e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several environmental settings proposed to concentrate, assemble, or copy primitive RNA-like molecules on the early Earth. Across the panels, the main idea is that physical environments can repeatedly bring strands together, separate them, and favor reactions without modern enzymes.\n\nDifferent settings are shown as simple cartoons: evaporating ponds concentrate dissolved material as water is lost; hydrothermal vents create circulation between cold and warm zones that appears to drive cycling of single-stranded and double-stranded RNA; freezing forms brine channels between ice crystals where solutes become concentrated; and heated rock pores provide small cavities with temperature gradients that could trap and recirculate molecules.\n\nOther panels emphasize surfaces and external energy sources. Mineral surfaces seem to adsorb strands and bring them into contact, while UV light and atmospheric CO2 are depicted as possible drivers of chemical activation or strand modification. Aerosols represent tiny droplets that can enclose molecules in air–water interfaces, and coacervates show droplet-like compartments that gather polymers into membrane-free protocell-like spaces.\n\nTaken together, the image compares multiple plausible microenvironments that could promote prebiotic chemistry through concentration, compartmentalization, and thermal or physical cycling.",
